# What NOT to upload to Vercel.
#
# `vercel --prod` uploads the working directory, not the Git tree -- so anything
# in .gitignore has to be repeated here or it ships to the builder anyway. The
# first deploy attempt failed at 1150 MB against a 500 MB function limit for
# exactly this reason: a dev machine's .venv, three node_modules trees and a
# Next.js build output are ~1.6 GB between them, and none of it is needed to
# answer a request.
#
# The serving stack is ~85 MB (onnxruntime + numpy). `vercel.json` states
# positively what the function does need -- {services,packages,apps/api,models}
# -- so this file only has to keep the weight off the upload.

# Python virtualenv. 718 MB locally, and the builder installs its own from
# requirements.txt.
.venv/
venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Node installs and framework build output. Vercel runs its own install and
# build for the console and bridge projects; uploading ours only slows it down
# and risks shipping platform-specific binaries built for Windows.
node_modules/
.next/
dist/
*.wasm.map

# Datasets, fetched by `python -m data.download`. Training reads them; serving
# never does -- the API loads models/fuel_degradation.onnx, which vercel.json
# includes explicitly. 95 MB saved.
data/

# THE DEPENDENCY MANIFESTS FOR DEVELOPERS -- deliberately withheld from the
# builder, and the reason the second deploy still failed at 1050 MB after the
# upload had already been cut to ~7 MB.
#
# The size was never the upload; it was what Vercel INSTALLED. Given a
# pyproject.toml it installs that project's dependencies, and this project's are
# the full development set: pandas, pyarrow, scikit-learn, xgboost,
# psycopg[binary]. That is the 358 MB training stack the ONNX export exists
# specifically to avoid shipping -- serving needs onnxruntime and numpy, ~85 MB.
# uv.lock pins the same set, so it has to go too.
#
# With both withheld, requirements.txt is the only manifest the builder can see,
# which is what it was written to be. Nothing breaks: api/index.py puts the repo
# root on sys.path, so `services`, `packages` and `apps` import as plain
# directories shipped by vercel.json's includeFiles -- the package is never
# pip-installed, on Vercel or locally.
pyproject.toml
uv.lock

# Secrets. These are set in the Vercel dashboard, never uploaded. Belt and
# braces alongside .gitignore, because this file governs a different transport.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Documentation, tests and screenshots are not part of any deployment.
docs/
tests/
*.png
*.jpg
*.pptx

# Local tooling
.vscode/
.idea/
.playwright-mcp/
